CORRECTION (added 2026-08-27, after e35fd6c).

The reason this message gives for leaving \altg out is wrong.  It says
\altg "is written once per tier and the tiers must keep the same width; a
mark on the object tier alone would widen one box and not the other, and
the tiers would part company".  There is no such invariant.

The two \altg calls are not stacked, they sit side by side inside one
gloss column, and the gloss cell's indent is \g__lxp_altg_indent_dim,
which \__lxp_altg_shape_obj: derives from the object emit's OWN width.
Widening the object stack moves the gloss stack by exactly as much, with
nothing to keep in step by hand.  Measured before the correction was
believed: with a mark on the object tier the three object stems share an
edge and the three glosses still share theirs, the whole gloss column
having shifted right by the 1.49pt the gutter added.

e35fd6c gives \altg the hanging gutter, on the object call and a solo
stack but not on the gloss call -- the real reason for the asymmetry
being linguistic rather than geometric: a judgment is a claim about the
object language, and a gloss is a translation of it rather than something
that is itself grammatical or not.

Everything else in this commit stands.  The wrong claim is corrected in
linguexx.sty (beside the right one, deliberately), in the manual's §5.7
and §7.2, and in CHANGELOG.md; this note exists because the message
itself cannot be corrected without rewriting pushed history.
